  "feedback": "# Message to Agent 1: Next Generation Curve Parameters\n\n## Analysis of Top 3 Performers\n\n**Best Curve: curve_2_v5.py (Score: 0.94)**\n- Achieved exceptional uniform sample density (0.99) and smoothness (0.924)\n- Uses ultra-dense serpentine with 300 lines\n- Key innovation: 2D sinusoidal perturbation with decoupled X and Y frequencies\n  - X perturbation: amplitude=0.012, frequency=18\n  - Y perturbation: amplitude=0.008, frequency=22\n- The asymmetric perturbation frequencies (18 vs 22) prevent resonance patterns\n\n**Second Best: curve_5_v5.py (Score: 0.845)**\n- Strong smoothness (0.912) and density (0.952)\n- Uses variable density with 150 lines and density_exponent=1.5\n- Minimal perturbation (amplitude=0.008, frequency=15)\n- The power-law spacing (t^1.5) creates adaptive coverage\n\n**Third Best: curve_4_v5.py (Score: 0.82)**\n- Excellent density (0.976) through hybrid approach\n- Combines 150-line serpentine with 30-turn quantized spiral\n- Uses discrete angle quantization (100 steps) for spiral portion\n\n## Key Success Patterns Identified\n\n1. **High line density** (150-300 lines) consistently produces better results\n2. **Small perturbations** (0.008-0.012 amplitude) improve smoothness without destroying uniformity\n3. **Prime or coprime frequencies** (15, 18, 22) avoid aliasing artifacts\n4. **2D perturbation** outperforms 1D perturbation significantly\n5. **Hybrid approaches** show promise when properly balanced\n\n## Instructions: Generate 5 New Curve Variations\n\n### Variation 1: \"Optimized 2D Perturbation Serpentine\"\n- Base: 250 lines (between 150 and 300)\n- X perturbation: amplitude=0.010, frequency=17 (prime)\n- Y perturbation: amplitude=0.009, frequency=23 (prime)\n- Test whether odd primes further reduce artifacts\n\n### Variation 2: \"Triple-axis Perturbation Serpentine\"\n- Base: 300 lines\n- X perturbation: amplitude=0.011, frequency=19\n- Y perturbation: amplitude=0.007, frequency=29\n- Add correlation term: `x += 0.004 * np.sin(13 * np.pi * (x + y))`\n- Explore coupled XY perturbation for emergent patterns\n\n### Variation 3: \"Adaptive Density with 2D Perturbation\"\n- Base: 200 lines with density_exponent=1.8 (higher than 1.5)\n- X perturbation: amplitude=0.010, frequency=16\n- Y perturbation: amplitude=0.008, frequency=20\n- Combine the best of curve_2 and curve_5\n\n### Variation 4: \"Phase-Shifted Dual Serpentine\"\n- First half: 150 lines, standard serpentine with perturbation (amp=0.009, freq=17)\n- Second half: 150 lines, serpentine starting at y=0 but phase-shifted by \u03c0\n- Both halves use 2D perturbation with different frequency pairs\n- Smooth transition at midpoint using sigmoid blending\n\n### Variation 5: \"Ultra-Dense Low-Perturbation Serpentine\"\n- Base: 400 lines (push density higher)\n- Minimal 2D perturbation: X amp=0.006, freq=21; Y amp=0.005, freq=19\n- Test whether ultra-high density can maintain smoothness with smaller perturbations\n\n## Parameter Constraints\n- Keep all perturbation amplitudes in range [0.005, 0.012]\n- Use prime or coprime frequencies between 13-31\n- Maintain num_lines between 150-400\n- Always apply np.clip(x, 0, 1) and np.clip(y, 0, 1) for boundary safety\n-"
